## 3. Target Architecture

### 3.1 Layered model — single owner per concern

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Transport layer — useChatSession                             │
│  • IPC subscription, buffer replay, send/interrupt           │
│  • Owns: event ordering, batching, dedup                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │  (batched events, max 1/frame)
              ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ State layer — chatTimelineReducer (immutable, structural)    │
│  • Pure reducer; uses Immer for structural sharing           │
│  • Stable item identity (id) + content version (rev)         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │  TimelineItem[]
              ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Selector layer — useChatSelectors                            │
│  • finalOnly filter, search-match indices, derived counts    │
│  • Memoized per (timeline ref, options)                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │  DisplayItem[]
              ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ View layer — ChatTimeline (single owner of scroll + virt)    │
│  • One library: react-virtuoso (or hand-rolled fallback)     │
│  • Owns scroll position, follow-output, anchoring on prepend │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
              │  visible items
              ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Renderer layer — pure, memoized, per-kind                    │
│  • <UserMessage>, <AssistantText>, <ToolCall*> …             │
│  • React.memo with (item.id, item.rev) equality              │
│  • Markdown rendered through cached AST cache (LRU)          │
│  • Tool output passes through sanitize + ANSI normalize      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### 3.2 Key invariants

1. **Stable identity**: Every TimelineItem has `id` (stable) and `rev` (monotonic content version). Memo equality uses `(id, rev)` only — never deep compare.
2. **One scroll owner**: `ChatTimeline` is the only place that reads or writes scroll position. No other component touches `scrollRef`.
3. **One render per frame**: All deltas arriving within a frame are coalesced into a single dispatch. The reducer never sees more than one delta per `(messageId, blockIndex)` per frame.
4. **Partial parse never blanks**: A tool whose JSON input is mid-stream renders a *progressive skeleton* (tool name + spinner + best-effort key list), never empty/null.
5. **Bounded item height contributes bounded jank**: estimate is per-kind, not a global constant. Initial paint is within ±20% of measured size.
6. **Output is sanitized at the boundary**: Tool output / stderr passes through ANSI-strip + DOMPurify allowlist before reaching markdown.
7. **No nested scroll inside the virtual list**: Long tool output uses "Show all (N lines)" expand instead of an inner scroller.

### 3.3 Library decision

Two viable paths — both end with a single scroll/virt owner:

| Option | Pros | Cons | Recommendation |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. `react-virtuoso`** | Battle-tested chat virtualization. `followOutput` + `firstItemIndex` + `atBottomStateChange` integrated. Handles dynamic heights w/o measure conflicts. | New dep. Some streaming edge cases (rapid mutations of last item) need care. | **Chosen** for Phase 2. |
| **B. Hand-roll** on top of tanstack-virtual + own follow-output | Zero new deps. Full control. | More code to own forever. Stick-to-bottom logic is subtle (anchor on prepend, smooth-follow, etc.). | Fallback if Virtuoso shows blockers. |

Decision: start with Virtuoso. If we hit a blocker, we keep the layered architecture and swap the view layer only — the rest is library-agnostic.

